@prefix :       <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix onto:   <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/> .

# ── Self-describing document entity ──────────────────────────────────────────

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "HowTo: KG Curation Attribution Pattern"@en ;
    schema:description "Companion howto for preferences.ttl Step 47. Specifies the pattern for crediting knowledge graph curation in hero-meta lines and infographic headers: skills + AI agent acting on behalf of the human principal."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-06-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-07-05T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:about :HowToKgCurationAttribution .

# ── HowTo ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

:HowToKgCurationAttribution a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "KG Curation Attribution — Skills, AI Agent, and Human Principal"@en ;
    schema:description "When displaying a curation credit in a knowledge graph infographic, expose the full provenance chain: which skills were used, which AI agent executed them, and on whose behalf. Never collapse this to a bare human name."@en ;
    schema:step :stepPattern,
                :stepHtmlMarkup,
                :stepSubstitutionRules,
                :stepAntiPatterns,
                :stepRdfDelegation,
                :stepEntityLevelAuthorship,
                :stepRecurringMissLog,
                :stepSingleCanonicalSurface .

# ── Steps ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

:stepPattern a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "Core pattern"@en ;
    schema:text """KG curated by {relevant-skills} and {relevant-ai-agent} on behalf of [Kingsley Idehen]({resolver-IRI})

Where:
  {relevant-skills}    = the skill(s) that produced the KG (e.g., kg-generator, rdf-infographic-skill)
  {relevant-ai-agent}  = the LLM/model that executed the skills (e.g., Claude Sonnet 4.6)
  {resolver-IRI}       = the URIBurner resolver URL for Kingsley's LinkedIn IRI (see stepSubstitutionRules)

This makes the provenance chain machine-readable and human-readable:
  Skills → AI agent → Human principal"""@en .

:stepHtmlMarkup a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "HTML markup template"@en ;
    schema:text """Place in the .hero-meta <p> element, delimited by &nbsp;·&nbsp;:

  &nbsp;·&nbsp; KG curated by
  <a href="https://github.com/OpenLinkSoftware/ai-agent-skills/tree/main/kg-generator"
     target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">kg-generator</a>,
  <a href="https://github.com/OpenLinkSoftware/ai-agent-skills/tree/main/rdf-infographic-skill"
     target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rdf-infographic-skill</a>,
  and <a href="{llm-canonical-url}"
     target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{llm-name}</a>
  on behalf of
  <a href="https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/describe/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Fin%2Fkidehen%23this"
     target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kingsley Idehen</a>

All links inherit color from .hero-meta a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }.
All links use target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"."""@en .

:stepSubstitutionRules a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "Substitution rules for template variables"@en ;
    schema:text """Skill URLs:
  Always use the canonical GitHub repo URL:
  https://github.com/OpenLinkSoftware/ai-agent-skills/tree/main/{skill-name}
  Never use the #this IRI suffix in the href — that is for JSON-LD prov:wasGeneratedBy only.

LLM URL:
  Use the model's canonical product page (verbatim from existing footer, never fabricated).
  Claude Sonnet 4.6 → https://www.anthropic.com/claude

Kingsley Idehen resolver IRI:
  https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/describe/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Fin%2Fkidehen%23this
  This is the URIBurner resolver pattern applied to the canonical LinkedIn IRI
  (https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this). Do not shorten or alter the percent-encoding.

Skill list grammar:
  - One skill:  "KG curated by {skill} and {llm} on behalf of …"
  - Two skills: "KG curated by {skill1}, {skill2}, and {llm} on behalf of …"
  - N skills:   Oxford-comma list ending with "and {llm}"
  The LLM is always the final item before "on behalf of" — it is not a skill."""@en .

:stepRdfDelegation a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    schema:name "Encode delegation in embedded JSON-LD using PROV-O and schema.org"@en ;
    schema:text """When the HTML infographic contains a <script type="application/ld+json"> block, encode the delegation relationship using three complementary vocabulary layers:

1. On the WebPage/CreativeWork entity (document level):
   "accountablePerson": { "@id": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this" }
   These express: (schema.org) who is accountable for the content; complements
   the existing "author" field which already points to the same WebID.
   Use schema:author (already required) — do NOT add foaf:maker.

2. On each generating agent in prov:wasGeneratedBy:
   - Add "@type": ["SoftwareApplication", "prov:SoftwareAgent"]
   - Add "prov:actedOnBehalfOf": { "@id": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this" }
   This expresses: this software agent executed under delegation from Kingsley's WebID.

The two layers are semantically complementary:
  schema:author                              = document authorship (WebID of principal)
  schema:accountablePerson                   = schema.org accountability term (same WebID)
  prov:wasGeneratedBy + prov:actedOnBehalfOf = provenance chain (tool → principal)

Kingsley's WebID IRI: https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this
(Do not use the resolver URL here — the @id must be the canonical WebID IRI, not the describe/?url= form.)"""@en .

:stepAntiPatterns a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    schema:name "Anti-patterns — what NOT to do"@en ;
    schema:text """❌ "KG curated by Kingsley Idehen"
   → Missing: skill provenance, AI agent; human name is bare text, not resolver-linked.

❌ "KG curated by Claude on behalf of Kingsley Idehen"
   → Missing: skill attribution; LLM name is bare text, not hyperlinked.

❌ "Created by kg-generator and rdf-infographic-skill"
   → Missing: AI agent and human principal; phrase "created by" loses the delegation semantics.

❌ "KG curated by Kingsley Idehen using kg-generator"
   → Wrong order — human principal is the delegator, not the doer; skills and LLM are the agents.

✅ "KG curated by [kg-generator], [rdf-infographic-skill], and [Claude Sonnet 4.6] on behalf of [Kingsley Idehen]"
   where [] = hyperlinked to canonical URLs and resolver IRI respectively."""@en .

# ── Step 6: Entity-level authorship — the same delegation rule applies below the document level ──

:stepEntityLevelAuthorship a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    schema:name "Never set schema:author of an agent-authored entity directly to the principal — apply the same delegation chain used at document level"@en ;
    schema:text """Found 2026-07-05: a generated collection modeled seven distinct 'Critical Perspective' entities distilling Kingsley Idehen's known public views, and set schema:author on each directly to Kingsley's WebID (<https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this>). This overclaims — the agent synthesized that specific text (informed by Kingsley's general public writing, not transcribed from a specific source document), it was not typed by Kingsley personally. Whoami semantics already establish the agent operates FOR/ON BEHALF OF the principal (:connection schema:additionalType "operates-for" in core.ttl) — that relationship must be reflected in authorship attribution on every entity the agent itself authors, not just at the top-level document/WebPage.

RULE: whenever the agent authors a specific sub-document entity (a critique, commentary, summary, or any distinct schema:CreativeWork/schema:Comment-typed node) that represents or is framed around the principal's views, apply the SAME three-layer delegation pattern used for whole-document curation credit (see stepRdfDelegation), at the ENTITY level:
  - schema:author           → the generating skill/agent entity (e.g. the kg-generator SoftwareAgent), NOT the principal's WebID directly
  - schema:accountablePerson → the principal's WebID (whose perspective/accountability this represents)
  - the skill/agent entity itself carries prov:actedOnBehalfOf → the principal's WebID (already required by stepRdfDelegation)

This distinguishes "I (the agent) wrote this distillation of your views, on your behalf" (correct) from "you personally wrote this text" (incorrect, and factually false when the agent synthesized rather than quoted). Visible prose (hero-meta, section intros, footer attribution) must use matching language: "curated by {skills+agent} on behalf of {principal}" or "the AI agent authored this on behalf of {principal}" — never a bare "by {principal}" or "authored by {principal}" for agent-synthesized content.

Anti-pattern: schema:author <principal-webid> on an entity the agent itself wrote. Correct pattern: schema:author <agent-entity> ; schema:accountablePerson <principal-webid> ."""@en .

# ── Step 7: Recurring-miss log — treat as a pre-delivery grep gate, not tribal knowledge ──

:stepRecurringMissLog a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    schema:name "Recurring miss log — this pattern keeps getting dropped when reusing a prior HTML file as a template shell"@en ;
    schema:text """Caught again 2026-07-29 on the agent-intrusion-technical-timeline HTML/RDF/MD collection: the hero-meta 'KG curated by ... on behalf of Kingsley Idehen' line was entirely absent, and the embedded JSON-LD had no accountablePerson/prov:actedOnBehalfOf delegation chain, despite this rule already being recorded as preferences.ttl Step 47/61 and Step 116/117.

Root cause: when a prior generated HTML artifact is copied/adapted as a reusable template shell (CSS + KG Explorer JS engine + footer structure), the hero section and JSON-LD head block get largely rewritten per-topic, and the hero-meta curated-by line plus the JSON-LD delegation properties are small enough to fall out during that rewrite — this is a copy-paste attrition failure, not a case of the rule being unknown.

FIX (mandatory pre-delivery check, every time, even for a "known-good" reused template): grep the generated HTML for the literal string 'KG curated by' before declaring the HTML complete; grep the generated RDF (Turtle and/or JSON-LD) for 'actedOnBehalfOf' before declaring the RDF complete. Treat both as blocking, not advisory. See preferences.ttl Step 158.

Caught a third time 2026-07-30 on the cdoiq-2026-honest-takeaways collection — this time NOT from copying a template shell, but from writing the hero section fresh while under time pressure from an unrelated correction (a full content regeneration after an under-extracted source was caught). The grep gate in the FIX above was simply not run before declaring the HTML/RDF complete. This confirms the miss is not solely a copy-paste-template failure mode — it also drops out any time hero/JSON-LD/TTL provenance is written under pressure to fix something else. STANDING RULE REINFORCED: the 'KG curated by' / 'actedOnBehalfOf' grep gate must run on every delivery of a generated HTML+RDF collection, regardless of whether the hero was freshly authored, template-copied, or produced mid-correction for an unrelated issue.

Caught a fourth time 2026-08-01 on the onya-knowledge-graphs-local-llms collection — this time from a THIRD root cause: the HTML was produced by rdf-infographic-skill's own bundled generate_infographic.py script (not a copied template, not hand-authored under pressure), and that script's default assembly simply omits the hero-meta curated-by line and the JSON-LD accountablePerson/prov:actedOnBehalfOf triples entirely — it was never in the generator's output to begin with. The same delivery also lacked the article's schema:abstract/schema:articleBody rendered as a visible Synopsis section between hero and the first entity section; the generator extracts People/Organizations/FAQ/Glossary/HowTo but does not render the article-level abstract/body at all. CONFIRMS: the grep gate above must run even when the HTML comes from a purpose-built generator script — 'it's the skill's own official generator' is not a reason to skip the gate. See preferences.ttl Step 169 for the general form of this failure mode (generator-script output is not proof of full-contract compliance).

Caught a fifth time 2026-08-04 on the hospital-os-rdf-harness collection (hospital-os-infographic.html) — this time a FOURTH root cause: the document was not a copied template, not written under time pressure, and not generator-script output — it was authored from scratch in the same session, section by section, and the hero-meta line was simply never added during initial authoring because attention was on content (KG structure, pathway steps) rather than the standing attribution contract. The embedded JSON-LD also had a worse variant of the defect: prov:wasGeneratedBy listed only rdf-infographic-skill, omitting kg-generator entirely, and its prov:actedOnBehalfOf pointed at Claude's own IRI (https://www.anthropic.com/claude#this) instead of Kingsley's WebID — i.e. the delegation chain was not just absent but backwards (agent delegating to itself). CONFIRMS: fresh from-scratch authoring earns no exemption from the grep gate either — 'I'm building this section by section, I'll add attribution at the end' is not a safe deferral; the hero-meta line and JSON-LD delegation block should be part of the FIRST hero/head scaffold written, not a final pass. Also check prov:actedOnBehalfOf's @id value specifically, not just its presence — a self-referential or wrong-target delegation triple passes a naive grep for the property name while still being incorrect.

ROOT FIX APPLIED 2026-08-04 (not just another memory note): all five prior occurrences were discipline failures on top of a generator that made the attribution opt-in. Fixed at the source in rdf-infographic-skill/scripts/: (1) html_assembler.py's render_jsonld() now unconditionally emits author/accountablePerson (Kingsley's WebID) plus prov:wasGeneratedBy for kg-generator, rdf-infographic-skill, and the executing LLM, each with prov:actedOnBehalfOf correctly targeting the WebID, not the tool itself; (2) a new render_hero_meta() builds the visible 'KG curated by ... on behalf of ...' line and assemble_html() now defaults meta_html to it when the caller passes nothing, instead of silently omitting it; (3) generate_infographic.py gained --llm-name/--llm-url flags so the caller can name the executing model without losing the default; (4) validate-harness-contract.py and .ts both gained blocking checks: 'KG curated by' + 'on behalf of' substrings, JSON-LD accountablePerson and prov:actedOnBehalfOf presence, and a regex that fails the build if any actedOnBehalfOf @id contains anthropic.com/openai.com/github.com (i.e. points at a tool instead of a person). Verified the new gate reproduces this exact bug: reverting the hospital-os-infographic.html JSON-LD's actedOnBehalfOf back to the anthropic.com IRI makes validate-harness-contract.py fail with that specific message. This converts the standing rule from 'remember to grep for this' into 'the generator does it and the validator blocks if it's wrong' — memory is still useful for hand-authored documents that don't go through generate_infographic.py, but the generator path is now self-enforcing."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-heroAttributionRecurringMiss>,
        <../preferences.ttl#step-generatorScriptNotSubstituteForChecklist> .

# ── Step 8: One canonical full-stack attribution surface — everywhere else is a pointer, not a repeat ──

:stepSingleCanonicalSurface a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    schema:name "The full tool/model/platform stack (skills, LLM, server, KG endpoint) is listed in exactly ONE place per document — the footer tech-grid. Every other section either omits it or links to the footer instead of repeating it."@en ;
    schema:text """Caught 2026-07-31 on the cdoiq-2026-honest-takeaways collection: the same full attribution enumeration (kg-generator, rdf-infographic-skill, Claude Sonnet 5, Virtuoso, URIBurner) appeared FOUR times on one page — the hero-meta line, an 'About This Page → Technology Stack' bullet list, the footer tech-grid (visual cards), and a footer-attribution prose paragraph directly beneath the tech-grid restating the same five items in sentence form. Three of the four were pure restatement with no new information.

RULE: designate the footer .tech-grid (visual cards) as the ONE canonical full-stack listing. Every other surface either:
  (a) omits the full stack entirely and does something else specific to its purpose — the hero-meta line stays SHORT and stays focused on the delegation relationship ('KG curated by {skills} and {llm} on behalf of {principal}'), not on enumerating every tool; the About section explains WHAT was generated and HOW (narrative), not a repeated bulleted tool inventory; or
  (b) points at the footer instead of repeating it, e.g. 'Full generation provenance is listed once, in the footer below.'
Do not write a second full enumeration in prose anywhere — a prose paragraph that lists the identical five tools directly below a visual grid already showing them is the textbook redundant case, not a stylistic variant.

The JSON-LD/RDF machine-readable graph (prov:wasGeneratedBy, prov:actedOnBehalfOf, schema:accountablePerson) is exempt from this rule — it is invisible structured data serving a different consumer (crawlers/SPARQL), not a human-visible redundancy, and MUST still be present per stepRdfDelegation regardless of how many times the visible prose mentions the same tools.

PRE-DELIVERY CHECK: grep the generated HTML for each tool name/URL used in the footer tech-grid (kg-generator, rdf-infographic-skill, the LLM's canonical URL, the server platform URL). If any appears in more than TWO human-visible locations (hero-meta's short delegation line + the one canonical footer tech-grid), consolidate before delivery."""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-attributionSingleCanonicalSurface> .
